  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Giens peninsula, in Hyères, is ringed by a coastal path that runs along wild coves, with sea views at every turn. It is managed by the Métropole Toulon Provence Méditerranée (TPM) and crosses a Conservatoire du littoral site, a protected Mediterranean forest. The TPM topoguide describes two loops on the peninsula: the loop around La Madrague harbour (3 h) and the walk between La Badine and Darboussières beach (8 h, sporty), with steep cliff-edge sections.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>For your dog, the rule is clear: the coastal path topoguide published by the TPM Métropole (online version of 24 July 2026), which the town of Hyères relays on its website, states "Dogs are banned on the coastal path and in the Port-Cros National Park". This ban applies to the loop around the Giens peninsula as it does to the other towns of the network (Six-Fours, La Seyne, Toulon...). The peninsula leaflet (2023 edition) also states that dogs are banned on the beaches. The massif is closed on red or extreme-red alert days for fire risk; access to the massifs should be checked on the official map in summer (21 June-20 September).</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: out of respect for the TPM rules, this path is not a place to walk your dog; head instead to the <a href="/en/fiche-hyeres-merou">Mérou dog beach</a> in Hyères.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Hyères and its beaches are nearby, notably the <a href="/en/fiche-hyeres-merou">Mérou dog beach</a>. On site, a <a href="/en/fiche-toiletteur-pat-toutou">groomer</a> and the <a href="/en/fiche-camping-mediterranee-hyeres">Camping Méditerranée</a> round out a stay in the area nicely.</p>
    </div>

